.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1243 AM PDT Wed Jul 29 2026
(Today and tonight)

Interesting water vapor imagery overnight with a few weather
features of note: impressive upper level ridge with monsoon
moisture sparking storms over the Desert SW into the Rockies, a
major hurricane Genevieve about 600 mi SW of Baja, and a weak dry
slot shortwave trough sweeping through CA. While dry at 400mb
over the Bay Area satellite fog product tells a different story.
While not continuous, a much more robust marine layer is present
tonight with stratus expanding over the coastal waters and
beginning to move inland.

Morning inland stratus will quickly fade and erode back to the
coast by mid-morning. Today will be a preview of what`s to come
the next few days: fading marine layer by mid-morning with
warming inland temps. Wouldn`t be surprised one bit if stratus
lingering along the coast for much of the day. Temperatures this
afternoon will be similar to Tuesday along the coast/bays with
highs 60s/70s (a few low 80s) and away from the coast/marine layer
influence temps will creep upwards with highs in the 80s to near
100.

One thing that will remain the same this afternoon will be the
winds. Given the temp difference from the coast to inland onshore
flow will bring breezy to gusty winds again. Winds will be
strongest this afternoon/early evening with gusts 30-40 mph.

For tonight: despite building 500mb heights and some areas of
offshore flow we will see the marine layer again with coastal
clouds moving inland.  Thermal belts will remain active too.

