.LONG TERM...
Issued at 115 AM PDT Wed Jul 29 2026
(Thursday through Tuesday)

The big highlight for the long term will be the building inland
heat. Thankfully, onshore flow and lingering marine layer
influence will keep coastal areas in check temp wise. The large
upper level ridge currently sitting well to the east will build
westward Thursday into the weekend. Generally speaking the
forecast remains on track with warm to hot conditions across the
interior with the hottest days being Saturday and Sunday.
Confidence still remains high enough to keep some marine layer
influence limiting heat potential near the coast. One change to
tonight`s forecast update are the temps for Saturday across the
interior, which have trended down. Still over 100 degrees, but
not shooting for 110 across interior Central Coast. This drop did
temper HeatRisk values a little bit. Regardless of that trend,
still expecting Moderate HeatRisk to develop across the interior
Thursday and persisting through at least Sunday. If you have
outdoor plans Thursday through Saturday across the interior be
prepared and follow some basic heat safety tips: Stay hydrated,
Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles, and limit
activities during peak heating. The slight downward temp trend
could also be attributed to some beefy NW winds over the coastal
waters this weekend. ECMWF EFI highlights the strong winds over
the coastal waters, which could help to enhance upwelling.

Temperatures will ease a bit late Sunday through late Monday as
the large upper ridge flexes south with a passing trough to the
north. The upper level ridge rebuilds by Tuesday into Wednesday
causing temperatures to rise again. Longer guidance and the CPC
outlook shows another round of even hotter temps will be possible
around August 5 through August 8.

